WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.816
Why agent code needs a different kind of review.

2
00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:09.432
Reviewing a colleague’s pull request and reviewing an agent’s diff look the same on screen, but the risks differ.

3
00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:13.637
Agents produce more code, faster, than people review comfortably.

4
00:00:13.917 --> 00:00:20.253
The code is usually plausible: well formatted, idiomatic, with confident comments and a tidy summary.

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:35.296
And the failure modes are distinctive: doing more than asked, quietly weakening a safeguard to make something work, adding a dependency, satisfying a test rather than the requirement, or following an instruction that came from a file rather than from you.

6
00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:45.880
The DORA 2025 report’s central finding is a useful frame: AI acts as an amplifier of an organization’s existing strengths and weaknesses.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:53.691
A team with disciplined review and good tests gets faster; a team that rubber-stamps gets more unreviewed code, faster.

8
00:00:53.971 --> 00:00:56.189
Review is where that difference is decided.

9
00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:07.563
This lesson gives you a checklist built for agent diffs, a reading order that makes large diffs tractable, honest guidance on model reviewers, and the security picture specific to agents.
